{"type":"standard","title":"Yitha Yitha language","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Yitha Yitha language</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q8053879","titles":{"canonical":"Yitha_Yitha_language","normalized":"Yitha Yitha language","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Yitha Yitha language</span></span>"},"pageid":32607946,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/Aboriginal_Tribes_of_Riverena.png","width":318,"height":305},"originalimage":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/Aboriginal_Tribes_of_Riverena.png","width":318,"height":305},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1330718816","tid":"25d50585-e781-11f0-878c-366d7b3cd351","timestamp":"2026-01-02T02:17:12Z","description":"Australian Aboriginal language","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yitha_Yitha_language","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yitha_Yitha_language?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yitha_Yitha_language?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Yitha_Yitha_language"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yitha_Yitha_language","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Yitha_Yitha_language","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yitha_Yitha_language?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Yitha_Yitha_language"}},"extract":"Yitha-Yitha is a moribund language of southern New South Wales spoken by the Yitha Yitha people. The language was studied in the 1980s. Yita Yita has many monosyllabic words, consonant finals and consonant clusters. Many Yita Yita place names include the words tin meaning foot, and cabul meaning leg.","extract_html":"<p><b>Yitha-Yitha</b> is a moribund language of southern New South Wales spoken by the Yitha Yitha people. The language was studied in the 1980s. Yita Yita has many monosyllabic words, consonant finals and consonant clusters. Many Yita Yita place names include the words <span><i lang=\"xth\">tin</i></span> meaning foot, and <span><i lang=\"xth\">cabul</i></span> meaning leg.</p>"}